  "rules": {
    "physical_key_registration_chain_v1": "A membrane keyboard closes a normally open circuit when pressure brings conductive layers into contact and bridges the gap, after which the parent device can register the keypress. A keyboard matrix connects switches at row-column intersections; the controller scans the matrix, identifies the pressed row-column combination, and generates the corresponding code. The matrix source describes a binary on-or-off signal and possible ghosting when diode or controller handling is limited. These stages separate key feel and contact construction from downstream detection. The frozen material does not provide a controlled latency or accuracy comparison proving that every mechanical keyboard registers faster or makes every user more accurate than a membrane keyboard.",
    "cherry_mx_color_scope_boundary_v1": "Within the documented Cherry MX family, the source groups Red under linear, Brown under tactile non-clicky, and Blue under tactile clicky, and its table gives different forces and actuation points. It says Cherry MX switches are often referenced by stem color, while other keyboard makers may use Cherry switches or imitate them; it also records linear, tactile, and clicky Grey variants. The catalog snapshots separately use OUTEMU, unspecified, and Gateron switch labels, including Gateron Yellow, Orange, and Pink. Therefore red, brown, and blue can be reported only as Cherry MX shorthand here. Switch colors are not a cross-manufacturer universal standard, and the behavior of every switch cannot be inferred from color alone; exact manufacturer, series, model, force curve, sound, and feel must be checked.",
    "scoped_transition_experience_v1": "One long-time Mac-like and Logitech Craft user says they have not tried a mechanical keyboard and asks whether quiet operation, a numpad, no soldering, Mac and Windows support, and accuracy expectations can be met. A Cherry MX Brown user reports that Brown felt less tactile than their laptop and membrane Logitech K120 and says they would not buy without a force chart. A first-time mechanical-board builder reports that Tealio linears felt excellent but that an overly sensitive W switch had to be desoldered and replaced. A fourth author describes an incomplete attempt to collect switch specifications across Gateron and smaller manufacturers. These are useful counterexamples and requirement prompts, not controlled comparisons, prevalence estimates, or proof of a universal mechanical advantage.",
    "first_keyboard_configuration_matrix_v1": "The frozen catalog pages are configuration snapshots. One advertises a full-size 104-key wired hot-swappable OUTEMU board with red or blue options at 49.99 dollars; one advertises a 61-key Bluetooth and USB-C compact board with brown or blue options at 49.99 dollars and no posted reviews; one advertises a wired 68-key compact board with a blue-switch configuration at 34.99 dollars; and one advertises a wired 61-key hot-swappable board with Gateron Yellow, Orange, or Pink options at 89.99 dollars. Compare needed keys and layout, operating-system and connection support, room-noise limit, exact switch maker and model, force and tactile or click behavior, hot-swap or soldering tolerance, price, and return terms separately. Seller ratings and claims do not establish independent quality, durability, latency, or person-specific fit.",
    "trial_first_purchase_boundary_v1": "Before buying, define non-negotiable keys and layout, wired or wireless devices, noise limit, preferred force and feedback, hot-swap needs, budget, and acceptable assembly or repair work. Identify the exact switch manufacturer, family, model, and specifications instead of generalizing a color. Try a switch sample when possible, but also test the complete board because the case, plate, keycaps, stabilizers, firmware, and layout affect the experience. During a return window, test every required key and shortcut, repeated and simultaneous inputs, connection switching, wake and reconnect behavior, noise in the actual room, and a sustained typing session. Use the result only for that exact configuration and user.",
    "evidence_bounded_membrane_to_mechanical_decision_v1": "Explain the supported contact-to-controller registration chain and the Cherry MX color shorthand with its manufacturer boundary. Do not promise that mechanical is universally faster, more accurate, quieter, more tactile, or better. Treat the conflicting community reports as motivation for an exact switch and complete-board trial. Select a returnable configuration only when its layout, connectivity, noise, exact switch, hot-swap, and budget properties fit the buyer; otherwise keep the current keyboard or defer. No universal best switch, board, or membrane-versus-mechanical winner is supported by the available material."
  },
